"# aos-gdb: stop an agent when a condition holds, look, go on\n\n`aos-gdb NAME` opens Python with `import aos.gdb as g` and `a = g.attach(NAME)` done (`--daemon D` or `$AOS_DAEMON`). Two layers: `a.xxx` is `aos-agent xxx` (`a.say`, `a.listen`, `a.ctl`, `a.state`, `a.agent`) plus reading the stack (`a.where`, `a.history`, `a.observed`, `a.file`); everything about breakpoints is under `a.bp`, built around the breakpoint: add, rm, set, ls, get, each by an id (a string such as `before_tool_1`).\n\nTwo kinds of breakpoint, one set of ids. A trigger is an ordinary hook on that agent (its own `<stack>/hooks.json`, name `gdb_<id>`, handler `lib/tools/agent/gdb_trigger`). A watcher (`on=\"watch\"`) is a separate proc on the daemon table (name `gdbw_<id>`) that looks at the agent's files and pauses it.\n\nHow it stops depends on the event, not on an option:\n\n- `before_tool`, `before_queue` (not happened yet): the trigger **holds** the agent inside its hook. The tool has not run; the table still says running, on purpose: aos-agent and the daemon do not know about gdb (kept decoupled), so use `a.bp.why()` / `a.bp.held()` to see a breakpoint stop. `a.bp.resume()` lets that call run as it was; `a.bp.skip()` skips the tool (the brain gets a \"skipped at a breakpoint\" line); `aos-agent ctl resume` from a shell also lets it run.\n- other events (`after_tool`, `brain_failed`, ...) and watchers: `aos-agent ctl pause`; the loop stops after the current step (after_tool: the result is in history, the brain has not seen it). `a.bp.resume()` = `ctl resume`.\n\n```python\nb = a.bp.add(\"before_tool\", tool=\"write\")          # held before write runs; b == \"before_tool_1\"\na.bp.add(\"after_tool\", tool=\"write\"); a.bp.add(\"tool_error\", times=2)\na.bp.add(\"after_tool\", sh=\"grep -q secret || exit 10\")             # event JSON on stdin; exit 0 = hit, 10 = no hit\na.bp.add(\"before_tool\", py=lambda ev: ev[\"arguments\"].get(\"path\", \"\").endswith(\".env\"), once=True)\na.bp.add(\"watch\", py=lambda t: len(t.history(0)) > 20, every_ms=500)   # t = the target's files; t.mem = a dict kept between looks\na.bp.add(\"watch\", also=[\"bob\"], sh=\"test -f flag || exit 10\", times=3)  # one watcher, two agents; cwd = each target's stack\na.say(\"hi\"); a.bp.wait_stopped(); a.bp.why(); a.bp.held(); a.where(); a.history(3)\na.bp.resume()                                       # takes no arguments; a.bp.skip(text=None) = let a held tool call go without running it\na.bp.ls(); a.bp.get(b); a.bp.set(b, enabled=False, times=3, once=True); a.bp.rm(b); a.bp.clear()\na.bp.stop(); a.bp.resume()                          # pause it now without a breakpoint, then go on\n```\n\n- `a.bp.get(id)` / `a.bp.ls()` rows: kind (trigger or watch), on, how (hold or pause), about, enabled, times, once, seen, fired, spent, last_error, and `here` (it is stopped at this one now). `set` changes enabled, times and once only; to change the condition or the event, add again with `name=ID, replace=True`.\n- While held: letters still queue; a `ctl pause` or `ctl stop` sent meanwhile applies after the tool ran (`why()` says so). `ctl sleep`, `rm` or a daemon shutdown cut the hold: the tool did not run, and the same call is held again when the agent comes back. Removing or disabling a breakpoint does not end a hold already on it.\n- Watchers are one step late (they poll). `times` = rounds in a row; after a stop the condition must be seen false once before it can stop again; paused or held targets are not checked. A broken watcher condition is `last_error`; a dead watcher does not touch its targets.\n- Triggers use `--on-error continue`: a broken condition or handler is one warning line, the agent does not stop or restart. `why()` lists notes since the last resume (`by`: hook, watch, repl, ctl), whether they belong to this stop, and `.breakpoint` = the id it is stopped at.\n- Python conditions run in another process: they see `json`, `re`, `os` and builtins; pass other values as defaults (`lambda ev, x=x: ...`). They are refused at once when they use outer variables or unknown names.\n- Do not put breakpoints on another agent unless you were asked to debug it: it stops that agent (aos/rules.json). More: `aos-gdb --help`, `help(a.bp)` in the REPL, proto8/py/aos/apps/gdb/README.md.\n"
